// REJECTED — A9, the converse: every key the `provides` clause declares must be
// installed by a `provide <key> { … }` block in the body. `S` declares
// `provides skin: Skin` and installs nothing. Before this rule the linker read
// the CLAUSE, so `S` was admitted as the provider of `skin`, `User` linked
// against it, and at run time `User` stayed PENDING (R2) with the failure
// reported one hop away — "no provider for key `out`" — from its cause. The
// one form that installs a declared key without a block is the multi-realm
// bind `isolate <key> in realms(...)` (the routes-only Router of
// stdlib/router.rvl), which is exempt by that syntax. The hint names both
// fixes: add the block, or drop the key from the clause.
//
// expected error:
//   a9_provides_without_block.rvl:17: `skin` is declared in the `provides` clause of S but no `provide skin { … }` block installs it (A9)

service Skin { fn name() -> Str }

component S provides skin: Skin {
  let x = effect Map.new() undo x.drop()
}

component User requires skin: Skin provides out: Skin {
  provide out { fn name() = skin.name() }
}
